  "query_preview": "..\n\n-  Valley rain and mountain snow expected through Monday morning,\n   focused along and south of I-90. Northwest Montana stays \n   mostly dry. \n\n-  High pressure bringing stable conditions and valley fog next\n   week. \n\n-  Potential return to unsettled pattern with chances for mountain\n   snow by next weekend (Nov. 7-8).\n\nWhat has changed with today's system? Not a whole lot. So far the\nrainfall amounts have ranged between 0.01 and 0.10\" south of \nHamilton. A fairly unexpected shortwave brought rain showers \namounting up to 0.24\" from McCall north and west to Lewiston, \nIdaho. Areas from Lemhi County east and north will receive the \nhighest precipitation amounts ranging from 0.25 to 0.40\" through \nMonday morning. Some snow accumulation is expected at the higher\nelevation mountain passes through tonight. \n\nWith skies partially clearing from northwest Montana southwards\nas the system shifts off to the east tonight, fog and low cloud\ndevelopment are possible. With not much mixing expected to occur\non Monday, the boundary layer moisture is expected to remain high\nthat by nightfall, and more fog or low clouds will be possible.\nRinse and repeat this several times through Wednesday. If clouds\nend up hanging around some locations, high temperatures could end\nup only getting into the mid to upper 40s rather than the 50s.\n\nA quick-moving shortwave trough will bring a chance for showers on\nThursday. Light snowfall accumulation is possible from the Idaho \nPanhandle to the northern Clearwater Mountains and across \nnorthwest Montana down to 5000 feet. There is a 25 to 35 percent \nchance for rain showers from central Idaho east and north to the \nGlacier Region. \n\nYet another trough passage may occur on Friday. Our clustering\ntool shows that at least 50% of the ensemble members have some \nsort of wave passage. After this, the clusters depict a very large\namplitude ridge encompassing the region. This would support above\naverage temperatures. As for precipitation, around 51% of the\nclusters show a flatter looking ridge which would support more\nclouds and possibly moisture moving into the Idaho Panhandle and\nnorthwest Montana by Sunday. The NBM, which has well over 100 \nweather model members from around the world, kind of supports this\nidea and has a 20 to 30% probability of precipitation of 0.01\" or\nmore occurring by the 8th. \n\nThe pattern has an El Nino look to it with deep troughing over \nthe northeast Pacific. This acts to advect milder air into the \nwestern U.S. The caveats for the Northern Rockies: We are getting \ninto the time of year when the effects of the sun become weaker \nand weaker. This may translate to the increase of low clouds and \nfog that may not dissipate very fast. While Pacific trough\npassages can give us sporadic precipitation, cloud cover can \nprevent us from getting to full potential for temperatures.",
